## Why This Is Asked

The problem mirrors real-world build systems and DAG validations, requiring both cycle detection and a valid execution order.

## Key Concepts

- DFS with 3-color marking to detect cycles
- Topological sort via postorder push
- Handling multiple components in a possibly disconnected graph

## Code Example

```javascript
function dfsTopo(graph) {
  const n = graph.length;
  const color = Array(n).fill(0); // 0 white, 1 gray, 2 black
  const order = [];
  const cycles = [];
  function dfs(u) {
    color[u] = 1;
    for (const v of graph[u] || []) {
      if (color[v] === 0) {
        if (dfs(v)) return true;
      } else if (color[v] === 1) {
        cycles.push([u, v]);
        return true;
      }
    }
    color[u] = 2;
    order.push(u);
    return false;
  }
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (color[i] === 0) {
      if (dfs(i)) break;
    }
  }
  return { topo: order.reverse(), cycle: cycles.length ? cycles : null };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you return the exact cycle path rather than just edge pairs?
- What are the time and space complexities for dense vs sparse graphs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical streaming pattern: sliding window analytics with per-user deduplication per category, real-time top-K, and lazy expirations to avoid full scans.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window with expirations
- Hash table for lastEvent per (user_id, category)
- Per-category max-heap with lazy deletions
- Global category-score heap for top-K queries

## Code Example
```javascript
class QuickCategoryGrouper {
  constructor(W){ this.W=W; this.last=new Map(); this.catSums=new Map(); this.catHeaps=new Map(); this.catTotal=new Map(); }
  addEvent(e){ const key=`${e.user_id}:${e.category}`; const prev=this.last.get(key); if(prev && prev.ts> e.ts-W){ this._applyDelta(e.category, e.value - prev.value); } else { this._applyDelta(e.category, e.value); }
    this.last.set(key,{value:e.value, ts:e.ts}); this._pushToHeap(e.category,{value:e.value, user:e.user_id, ts:e.ts}); }
  _applyDelta(cat, delta){ this.catTotal.set(cat,(this.catTotal.get(cat)||0)+delta); }
  _pushToHeap(cat, item){ if(!this.catHeaps.has(cat)) this.catHeaps.set(cat, []); this.catHeaps.get(cat).push(item); }
  topKCategories(K, currentTime){ // lazy pop to ensure validity
    const res=[]; for(const [cat, total] of this.catTotal){ res.push({cat, total}); }
    res.sort((a,b)=> b.total - a.total); return res.slice(0,K).map(r=>r.cat); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle skewed category distributions and ensure stability during microbursts?
- What are the trade-offs of using a single global heap vs per-category heaps for top-K queries?

## Why This Is Asked

This question adds counting of optimal partitions to a standard min-max DP, testing both correctness and edge-case handling in a realistic workload-balancing scenario.

## Key Concepts

- DP with a value-cost pair (minCost, count) and prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- Top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation with identical transitions
- Edge-case handling: n<k invalid, zeros, large sums

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostPartitions(nums, k){
  const n = nums.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+nums[i];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array.from({length:k+1},()=>null));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = {best: pref[i], count:1};
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let j=2;j<=Math.min(i,k);j++){
      let best = Infinity, cnt = 0;
      for(let t=j-1;t<=i-1;t++){
        const last = pref[i]-pref[t];
        const prev = dp[t][j-1];
        if(!prev) continue;
        const cand = Math.max(prev.best, last);
        if(cand < best){ best = cand; cnt = prev.count; }
        else if(cand === best){ cnt += prev.count; }
      }
      dp[i][j] = {best, count: cnt};
    }
  }
  const res = dp[n][k];
  return res ? {cost: res.best, ways: res.count} : null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify to return all partition indices achieving the min cost?
- Could you optimize to O(nk) time with a narrowed search if nums are non-decreasing?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical DP reasoning: variable step costs require deriving both a memoized and a tabular solution, handle unreachable states, and discuss trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Top-down DP with memoization
- Bottom-up DP (tabulation)
- Handling unreachable states with Infinity
- Time/space complexity: O(n|S|) time, O(n) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostTopDown(n, steps, memo=new Map()){
  if(n===0) return 0
  if(n<0) return Infinity
  if(memo.has(n)) return memo.get(n)
  let best = Infinity
  for(let s of steps){
    const c = s + minCostTopDown(n-s, steps, memo)
    if(c < best) best = c
  }
  memo.set(n, best)
  return best
}

function minCostBottomUp(n, steps){
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill(INF)
  dp[0] = 0
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for(let s of steps){
      if(i>=s && dp[i-s] !== INF){
        dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], s + dp[i-s])
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n] === INF ? -1 : dp[n]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if costs were a non-linear function cost(s) instead of s? 
- How would you extend to count the number of ways to reach n with min cost, not just the cost itself?

